  '@tflw/lang': 1475, // +17 in `M147d-6` (all in sessionEnvScope.test.ts, `M137f-02`/D642 — `session <name> for env <a>[, <b>...]`. The row reads *a top-level `session` has to resolve under every declared env*, and both halves reproduce: with `api adminConsole` on one env of two, `check --env one` is clean and `check --env two` is `TF026` **at the session line**, before any assertion, under an invocation that may name a single file with nothing to do with the console. Three things measuring it added, none of them in the row. **The runtime never establishes a session nobody names** — all five establishment paths key off `test.sessions`/`crawl.sessions`/`scenario.sessions`/`probePrincipalFor(name)` — so the checker was strictly stricter than the thing it checks. **The cost is the D21 declarations, not the service name**, which is what decides between the row's own two candidate fixes rather than taste: "let a session name an absolute origin" deletes the `api` line and leaves `TF060`'s affirmation and `TF065`'s `allow hosts` entry, and in the corpus this was filed from one env had to widen an allowlist whose entire purpose is to make a refusal have exactly one possible cause. **And a session is a probe-set member** (D306), so a session that resolves nowhere silently removes an identity from every Tier 2 differential oracle while the suite stays green — which is why a typo'd env is `TF074` rather than tolerated. Four of the seventeen assert the *absence* of the clause still means every env, because that one value is the whole additive claim and is one character away from turning an optional keyword into a breaking change. Two more pin the diagnostic's span to the name rather than the declaration, a `session` span running to the end of its indented body.) // +15 in `M147d-5` (all in pollableSubjects.test.ts, `A3-11`/D641 — which subjects a `wait until` may poll, and **seven of the fifteen are negatives** because the slice refuses more shapes than it admits. The old guard tested `subject.type !== 'LocatorSubject'` and so answered eight distinct mistakes with one sentence — a sentence naming the one spelling none of the eight was reaching for. Measuring the eight split them on a property already load-bearing in the runtime: five read the *response scope*, which one `api` step writes and nothing between two polls can change, and three (`page`, `request to \"…\"`, a value `of request to \"…\"`) are live browser observations `expect` has always re-read on a retry loop. **The row's own example is in the first group**, so `A3-11`'s capability request is the one shape the rule can prove is never worth admitting, while the gap it half-saw was real and three subjects wide. Two of the negatives are consequences the row never named: `matches snapshot` is the matcher half of the same rule and its only member (compared once against a baseline, so `wait until … matches snapshot` was a wait that could not wait), and `TF042` had never reached `wait until` at all — `expect button \"Go\" has no critical a11y violations` was `TF042` while the identical mistake one keyword over checked clean and failed mid-run, which is verbatim the scenario SPEC §1 cites as `TF042`'s reason to exist. Widening the subject set without that traversal would have shipped a *wider* unchecked matcher position, so it is part of the slice rather than a follow-up. One test asserts the negative case of the traversal itself: `checkMatcherSubjects` got its own walk rather than a case in `forEachExpectInSteps`, which two D21 safety layers also read.) // +17 in `M147d-4` (15 in waitBudget.test.ts + 2 DURATION_SITES rows, `A3-10`/D640 — a per-step wait budget, and **six of the fifteen are negatives**. The row said the api form of `wait until` takes a per-step timeout and the locator form does not; measuring what the api form's `timeout` *is* falsified it. That clause is `ApiRequestSpec.timeoutMs`, one poll's HTTP request budget, which decision 67 then clamps to what remains of the w